[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Anti-Patterns in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Object-Oriented Programming, design patterns provide proven solutions to recurring architectural problems. Conversely, anti-patterns represent common, counterproductive responses to these problems. Recognizing and refactoring anti-patterns like the God Object, Anemic Domain Model, and Spaghetti Code is crucial for maintaining scalable, readable, and robust software."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The God Object Anti-Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A God Object is a class that knows too much or does too much. It centralizes most of the system's business logic, violating the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP). Such a class typically has hundreds of methods and variables, making testing and maintenance extremely difficult."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// BAD: God Object managing users, orders, and database operations
class ApplicationManager {
    void processUser(String user) { /* ... */ }
    void calculateTax(double amount) { /* ... */ }
    void saveToDatabase(String query) { /* ... */ }
    void generatePDFReport() { /* ... */ }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Anemic Domain Model Anti-Pattern"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"An Anemic Domain Model occurs when domain objects contain state (properties and getters/setters) but no behavior. All business logic is extracted into procedural service classes. This treats objects merely as data structures, defeating the core philosophy of object-oriented encapsulation."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// BAD: Anemic Model with no behavior
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
    public void setBalance(double balance) { this.balance = balance; }
}

// Procedural logic handled elsewhere
class BankService {
    void withdraw(BankAccount account, double amount) {
        account.setBalance(account.getBalance() - amount);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encapsulate behavior inside the domain model instead of relying on external service classes."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure objects protect their own invariants (e.g., preventing a negative balance natively within the BankAccount class)."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Spaghetti Code Anti-Pattern"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spaghetti Code is characterized by an entangled, unstructured web of control flow and dependencies. It often features deep nesting, heavy use of global variables, and an absence of clear architectural boundaries, making the execution path nearly impossible to trace."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// BAD: Tightly coupled, unmaintainable control flow
class OrderProcessor {
    void process() {
        if (Session.isActive()) {
            if (Cart.hasItems()) {
                if (Inventory.checkStock()) {
                    // Nested deeply with side effects
                    System.out.println("Processing...");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By adhering to SOLID principles, enforcing strong encapsulation, and continuously refactoring code smells, developers can avoid these destructive anti-patterns and build maintainable, modular object-oriented systems."}]
